{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792939890,"nonce":"dFPhR9pfEf73x9FoWkJxNtaC7C4=","title":"Tiered: promote to LLVM when a function's expected remaining time exceeds the compile's cost","message":"The fixed quiet window (dad864bc) helps deltablue, meteor and spectral-norm and costs raytrace (1.105), go (1.077), SOR (1.058) and ai (1.028): the window is the same for a function about to run for seconds and for one that finishes in a few milliseconds. On a runner with two physical cores an LLVM compile takes a core from the program for 10 to 220 ms (zylua pipeline's main installs in 221 ms), so it pays only when the function will run long enough afterwards.\n\nWhat a decision could use, per function and per loop: calls or back-edge visits per millisecond since the baseline installed (the entry counters and probe counts the baseline keeps), the body's instruction count as a proxy for LLVM compile time, and the expected speed-up (vectorized loops gain little, call-bound loops little, scalar arithmetic loops much). Promote when rate times expected remaining time times the speed-up exceeds the compile estimate, with the remaining time estimated from the time already spent (a function that has run for t is assumed to run for about t more).","files":null}]}